 mean the prosecutors the defense  attorneys they've been they've been  going at it um and now they're in jury  deliberations and just to wait and see  what happens yeah and we just detailed  the five individual felony charges that  kyle rittenhouse faces um the most  severe one is first degree intentional  homicide which in some states would be  equivalent to first-degree murder  it's really among the most serious  charges and if convicted he could face  life in prison what's interesting is  that earlier this week the judge  dismissed a sixth charge against  rittenhouse which was possessing a  dangerous weapon as a minor because he  was 17 when the shootings happened and  he's now 18 years old so he's a legal  adult now wisconsin is an open carry  state so he was not doing anything wrong  by being armed in the state i'm just  curious when you were  at the courthouse with all the tension  there i mean  were there people talking about carrying  guns near the courthouse did you see  anyone carrying weapons  aside from the you know the officers who  are here at the courthouse i personally  have not  seen anyone um carrying a weapon i  haven't really heard any or i haven't  heard any language where you know some  of the protesters might be you know  saying oh they're gonna you know bring a  weapon or that someone they know is  going to bring a weapon i haven't heard  anything of that nature there's been  more just um  a really rowdy sense of dialect among  the two sides of the story where they're  verbally really going after one another  yeah almost like they're they're having  the argument with each other um  in a trial but not really because  they're outside i it's this has been  playing out for two weeks now and it  seems to really come down to whether he  was acting in self-defense or not or  whether he was committing serious crimes  what are people saying specifically i  know that you went around and talked  with a lot of different people to gather  their thoughts 